  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 339 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nStrong upper level ridging will continue into the weekend allowing \nfor surface high pressure to build south out of Canada keeping \nconditions dry through the first half of next week. As high pressure \nsettles to the southeast of Nebraska by Saturday, warm air advection \nwill begin to push milder air into the region. Temperatures will \nsteadily rise from the mid 60s to low 70s on Saturday into the 70s \nby Tuesday. These highs will easily be at least 5 degrees above \nnormals for this time of year. Overnight lows will also rise through \nthe middle of next week. Saturday lows will be almost 10 degrees \nwarmer than Friday night lows in the upper 30s to low 40s. Overnight \ntemperatures will rise back into the mid 40s to low 50s by the \nmiddle of the week. Again these lows will be well above normals \n(normal in the mid 30s). The next chance of some isolated \nprecipitation will arrive on Wednesday and Wednesday night as a weak \nshortwave trough pivots across western Nebraska. Confidence remains \nlow in timing and coverage of this system though, so nothing more \nthan some slight chance PoPs (less than 20 percent) are in the \nforecast at this time.",
